content.text:
The evidence points to one fault: extra time on the `search`→`rate` connection. I'm not naming a second root cause, because the other shifts I checked (CPU, restarts, logs, `user`/`geo` latency) are low or follow load rather than a fault.

```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "rate",
      "fault_kind": "network_delay",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name caller, s.service_name callee, s.span_name, count(*) n, round(approx_quantile(c.duration/1e6,0.5),2) client_p50_ms, round(approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.5),2) server_p50_ms, round(approx_quantile(c.duration/1e6,0.5)-approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.5),2) gap_p50_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON s.parent_span_id = c.span_id AND s.trace_id = c.trace_id WHERE c.attr_span_kind='Client' AND s.attr_span_kind='Server' AND c.service_name='search' AND s.service_name='rate' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "search's client calls to rate take about 1496 ms p50, but rate's server spans take 0.7 ms, a 1495 ms gap on the wire."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name caller, s.service_name callee, s.span_name, count(*) n, round(approx_quantile(c.duration/1e6,0.5),2) client_p50_ms, round(approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.5),2) server_p50_ms, round(approx_quantile(c.duration/1e6,0.5)-approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.5),2) gap_p50_ms FROM normal_traces c JOIN normal_traces s ON s.parent_span_id = c.span_id AND s.trace_id = c.trace_id WHERE c.attr_span_kind='Client' AND s.attr_span_kind='Server' AND c.service_name='search' AND s.service_name='rate' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "In the normal period the same search-to-rate hop had a gap of about 0.3 ms."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name caller, s.service_name callee, count(*) n, round(approx_quantile(c.duration/1e6,0.5)-approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.5),2) gap_p50_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON s.parent_span_id = c.span_id AND s.trace_id = c.trace_id WHERE c.attr_span_kind='Client' AND s.attr_span_kind='Server' AND c.service_name='search' AND s.service_name='geo' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Other search callees such as geo show a normal gap of about 0.35 ms, so the delay is specific to rate."},
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT service_name, attr_k8s_pod_name pod, metric, round(avg(value),1) avg_v, round(max(value),1) max_v FROM abnormal_metrics_sum WHERE service_name='rate' AND metric IN ('k8s.pod.network.errors','k8s.pod.network.io') GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "rate's pod network errors stay at zero and its network I/O stays near normal levels during the incident."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "rate", "to": "search",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT s.service_name callee, c.service_name caller, count(*) n, round(approx_quantile(s.duration/1e6,0.95),1) server_p95_ms FROM abnormal_traces s JOIN abnormal_traces c ON s.parent_span_id=c.span_id AND s.trace_id=c.trace_id WHERE s.service_name='search' AND s.attr_span_kind='Server' AND c.service_name='frontend' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "search server spans called by frontend now take about 2.7 s p95, inheriting the delay from its rate calls."}]},
    {"from": "search", "to": "frontend",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, count(*) n, round(approx_quantile(duration/1e6,0.95),1) p95_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='frontend' AND span_name='HTTP /hotels' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "frontend HTTP /hotels p95 rose to about 2762 ms, versus 7 ms in the normal period, with no errors."}]}
  ]
}
```

